>
  <p>
    A tenant improvement — a TI, or interior build-out — is the work that turns leased commercial space into a space a
    specific business can operate in. It might be a full build-out of an empty shell, or a reworking of a second-generation
    suite that used to be someone else’s office. Either way, the process follows the same path. Knowing it in advance is
    the easiest way to protect your schedule and budget.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-is-ti">What a tenant improvement is</h2>
  <p>
    Tenant improvements cover everything inside the leased premises that makes them fit for your use: walls and
    partitions, ceilings, doors and hardware, restrooms, flooring and finishes, plus the mechanical, electrical, plumbing
    and fire-protection changes that go with a new layout. The building’s structure, roof and shell are usually the
    landlord’s; what happens inside the demising walls is the TI.
  </p>
  <p class="callout">
    <strong>Shell vs. second-generation space.</strong> A “vanilla shell” or cold shell has little or nothing inside it.
    Second-generation space was built out for a previous tenant. Reusing walls, ceilings and restrooms in a
    second-generation space can save real money — if the existing layout and systems actually suit you.
  </p>

  <h2 id="lease">1. Read the lease before the floor plan</h2>
  <p>
    The lease decides who pays for what, who approves what, and who is allowed to build. Before you design anything,
    find out:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The TI allowance</strong> — how much the landlord contributes, how it is paid out, and what it can be spent on.</li>
    <li><strong>The work letter</strong> — the exhibit that divides landlord work from tenant work and describes the condition the space is delivered in.</li>
    <li><strong>Approval rights</strong> — most landlords review tenant drawings and contractors before work starts, and may require their own insurance limits.</li>
    <li><strong>Building rules</strong> — work hours, noisy-work windows, deliveries, elevator use and protection of common areas, especially in occupied buildings and shopping centers.</li>
    <li><strong>The rent commencement date</strong> — once rent starts, every week of construction costs you money. Your schedule should be built backward from it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="team">2. Pick a delivery method and team</h2>
  <p>There are three common ways to contract a build-out:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Design-bid-build / general contracting.</strong> An architect or designer completes the drawings, then a
      general contractor prices and builds them. Clear and familiar; best when the design is settled.{' '}
      <a href="/services/general-contracting/">More on general contracting</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Design/build.</strong> One team carries the project from concept to completion, with builders pricing the
      design as it develops. Best when the budget is fixed and the design isn’t.{' '}
      <a href="/services/design-build/">More on design/build</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Construction management.</strong> An experienced builder manages schedule, budget and quality on the owner’s
      behalf, often alongside contracts the owner holds directly.{' '}
      <a href="/services/construction-management/">More on construction management</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Bringing a contractor in early — even before drawings are finished — is one of the cheapest ways to avoid a budget
    surprise later.
  </p>

  <h2 id="design">3. Design and construction documents</h2>
  <p>
    Design moves from a test-fit (does the program fit the space?) to a floor plan, then to a full set of construction
    documents. A typical TI set includes demolition and new floor plans, a reflected ceiling plan, wall types, door and
    hardware schedules, finish plans, and mechanical, electrical, plumbing and fire-protection drawings. Food service,
    medical and other specialized uses add their own equipment and code requirements.
  </p>
  <p>
    The more complete and coordinated these documents are, the more accurate your price will be — and the fewer change
    orders you’ll see during construction.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pricing">4. Pricing and value engineering</h2>
  <p>
    A good estimate is more than a number. Ask for one that is built from the drawings line by line, with assumptions
    and exclusions written out. Then compare estimates scope-for-scope, not just total-for-total: a low number that
    excludes the ceiling grid or the restroom accessories isn’t actually low.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Value engineering</strong> means finding the materials, details and methods that deliver the function and
    quality you need for the best price. It works best during design, when changes are cheap — not after bids come in,
    when it often turns into cutting things you wanted.
  </p>

  <h2 id="permits">5. Permits and approvals</h2>
  <p>
    In Michigan, most build-outs need a building permit from the local building department (or from the state where a
    community doesn’t enforce its own code), plus separate trade permits for mechanical, electrical and plumbing work.
    The building department reviews the drawings for code compliance before issuing permits.
  </p>
  <p>Depending on the use, you may also need:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Health department plan review</strong> for restaurants and other food service establishments.</li>
    <li><strong>Fire marshal review</strong> of sprinkler, alarm and life-safety changes.</li>
    <li><strong>Landlord and, for franchises, franchisor approval</strong> of the drawings.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Plan review time varies by community, so submit as early as the drawings allow.</p>

  <h2 id="construction">6. Construction</h2>
  <p>A typical interior build-out runs in this order:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Demolition of anything not being reused.</li>
    <li>Layout of new walls on the floor, checked against the drawings.</li>
    <li>Framing — usually light-gauge metal studs — plus in-wall blocking for cabinets, grab bars and equipment.</li>
    <li>Rough-in of mechanical, electrical, plumbing and fire protection inside the open walls and ceiling.</li>
    <li>Rough inspections, then drywall hanging and finishing.</li>
    <li>Ceiling grid and tile, coordinated with lights, diffusers and sprinkler heads.</li>
    <li>Doors, frames and hardware; restroom partitions and accessories.</li>
    <li>Paint, flooring, millwork, fixtures and equipment.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Framing, drywall, ceilings and doors set the pace for most interiors — which is why{' '}
    <a href="/services/commercial-carpentry/">who performs the carpentry</a> matters as much as who manages the job.
  </p>

  <h2 id="closeout">7. Inspections, punch list and move-in</h2>
  <p>
    Before you can occupy the space, final inspections must pass and the building department must sign off — typically
    with a certificate of occupancy or completion. Your contractor should walk the space with you to build a punch list
    of remaining items, complete it, and hand over closeout documents such as warranties and as-built information.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mistakes">Mistakes that cost the most</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Designing before reading the work letter and TI allowance terms.</li>
    <li>Letting rent start before the schedule — including permits — is realistic.</li>
    <li>Comparing bids by total price instead of by scope.</li>
    <li>Changing the design after construction starts.</li>
    <li>Ordering long-lead equipment, fixtures or storefront glazing late.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you are planning a build-out, <a href="/contact/">send us your drawings or describe the space</a>. We’ll review
    it and tell you how we would deliver it.
  </p>
